09375, 0.00738525390625, -0.0281829833984375, 0.01007843017578125, 0.00281524658203125, -0.006809234619140625, 0.035675048828125, -0.01043701171875, -0.0195465087890625, 0.006744384765625, 0.037506103515625, 0.03594970703125, 0.0034961700439453125, 0.00601959228515625, -0.0079345703125, -0.0271759033203125, 0.01155853271484375, -0.02392578125, -0.004123687744140625, -0.0034122467041015625, -0.00933074951171875, 0.014068603515625, -0.001232147216796875, -0.0203399658203125, 0.027191162109375, -0.0212860107421875, 0.04449462890625, 0.01094818115234375, 0.0300445556640625, 0.039947509765625, -0.040130615234375, -0.027130126953125, 0.016632080078125, -0.00896453857421875, 0.0019550323486328125, -0.02215576171875, 0.0111236572265625, -0.00044989585876464844, -0.044189453125, 0.0086822509765625, -0.01306915283203125, 0.004352569580078125, 0.00028395652770996094, 0.02490234375, -0.00980377197265625, -0.00931549072265625, -0.0289459228515625, -0.00392913818359375, 0.011962890625, -0.01629638671875, 0.00019311904907226562, 0.006931304931640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "A volcano is a vent or fissure in the crust of a planetary-mass object that allows hot lava, volcanic ash, and gases to escape from a magma chamber below the surface.\n\nOn Earth, volcanoes are most commonly associated with tectonic plate boundaries. The majority of Earth's plate boundaries are underwater, and as a result, it is estimated that about 80% of the world\u2019s volcanoes are found beneath the oceans, particularly along mid-ocean ridges. For example, the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is a site of divergent plate boundaries, where tectonic plates move apart and magma rises to create new crust. Volcanoes at divergent boundaries, such as those along mid-ocean ridges, typically produce non-explosive eruptions.\n\nIn contrast, convergent plate boundaries occur where plates move toward each other, often resulting in one plate being forced beneath another in a process called subduction. This process leads to the formation of volcanoes that are often highly explosive, as seen in the Pacific Ring of Fire. The difference in eruption style between divergent and convergent boundaries is due to variations in magma composition and gas content.\n\nVolcanoes can also form in regions where the Earth's crust is stretching and thinning, even if these areas are not located at plate boundaries. Examples include the East African Rift, where the African plate is splitting into two; the Wells Gray-Clearwater volcanic field in Canada; and the Rio Grande rift in North America. In these rift zones, the thinning crust allows magma to reach the surface more easily.\n\nAdditionally, some volcanoes form away from plate boundaries through a process known as intraplate volcanism. This is typically attributed to mantle plumes\u2014columns of hot, buoyant rock rising from deep within the mantle, possibly originating near the core\u2013mantle boundary or the lower mantle. When a mantle plume reaches the base of the lithosphere, it can cause localized melting and create a hotspot. As a tectonic plate moves over a stationary hotspot, a chain of volcanoes can form, such as the Hawaiian Islands. Unlike volcanoes at plate boundaries, hotspot volcanoes are not directly related to the movement of tectonic plates.\n\nVolcanoes are usually not created at transform boundaries, where two tectonic plates slide past one another horizontally. This is because transform boundaries do not typically provide a pathway for magma to reach the surface, so volcanic activity is rare in these settings."}}